              "amount": 1,
              "unit": "tbsp",
              "prepNote": "toasted",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "soy-sauce-light",
              "amount": 1,
              "unit": "tsp",
              "prepNote": "to drizzle, optional",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Brew the green tea in a teapot or jug. Use 2 teaspoons of loose-leaf tea or 2 tea bags per 400ml of water just off the boil. Leave to steep for 2 minutes, then remove the tea."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the hot cooked rice between two deep bowls. Pack it loosely so the tea can soak in from all sides."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the hot tea or dashi slowly over the rice. It should cover about half the rice. Add more if you prefer a soup texture. Eat at once while the nori is still crisp."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the hot green tea slowly and evenly over each bowl. The rice should be about half submerged. Add a small drizzle of soy sauce if you like. Serve straight away."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Rice is too cold and the dish is not warming",
              "cause": "Leftover rice was not fully reheated before the tea was added.",
              "fix": "Reheat the rice fully in the microwave before building the bowl. The tea alone will not warm cold rice through."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Dish tastes bland",
              "cause": "The tea was too weak or the toppings do not have enough flavour.",
              "fix": "Use a stronger brew and add a drizzle of soy sauce. More boldly flavoured tsukemono pickles also help."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Nori has gone soft and slimy",
              "cause": "The nori sat in the hot tea for too long before eating.",
              "fix": "Ochazuke should be eaten straight away. Add the nori last and eat immediately. It softens quickly in liquid."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ochazuke has a long history in Japan. It appears in literature as far back as the Heian period. It was eaten as a way to use every last grain of rice without waste. Today it is eaten as a light lunch, a late-night snack or the final course of a meal. In some Japanese restaurants, guests are invited to end their dinner with a bowl of ochazuke to signal that the meal is finished."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}